46. GITHUB ACTIONS + APK BUILD

The project must include a working GitHub Actions Android APK build pipeline as part of this frontend phase.

Do not merely create a YAML file and claim CI is complete.

Actually configure and verify the workflow.

Required outcome

A GitHub Actions workflow must be able to:

GitHub push
    ↓
GitHub Actions
    ↓
Install dependencies
    ↓
Validate project
    ↓
Build Android APK
    ↓
Upload APK as workflow artifact

The workflow should build a debug APK for development/testing.

If the project structure supports it cleanly, also prepare the configuration so a release APK can be added later without redesigning the CI pipeline.

Requirements

Create the appropriate workflow under:

.github/workflows/

Use the project's actual framework and package manager.

The workflow should:

- Check out the repository
- Set up the required Node.js version
- Set up Java/Android tooling when required
- Install dependencies using the project's lockfile/package manager
- Run the appropriate frontend validation/build checks
- Build the Android APK
- Fail correctly if the build fails
- Upload the generated APK using GitHub Actions artifacts
- Give the artifact a clear name such as:

veyra-debug-apk

Do not hard-code machine-specific paths.

Do not rely on the developer's local computer.

Do not require paid CI services.

Use GitHub Actions wherever possible.

Expo / React Native

If the project is Expo/React Native, inspect the actual project configuration first.

Use the appropriate Android build method for the project.

Do not blindly assume a particular Expo workflow.

If EAS is required, configure it correctly and document exactly what is needed.

If a local Gradle build is more appropriate for the current project structure, use that instead.

The final workflow must correspond to the actual repository.

Build verification

After creating the workflow:

1. Inspect the generated Android project/configuration.
2. Run the relevant local validation/build checks if the environment allows it.
3. Commit the workflow to the repository.
4. Push the changes to the current working branch.
5. Trigger the GitHub Actions workflow.
6. Inspect the workflow result.
7. Fix any build errors.
8. Re-run until the workflow succeeds.
9. Confirm that the APK artifact is actually generated.

Do NOT say:

«"GitHub Actions has been configured"»

unless the workflow has actually been created and verified.

Do NOT say:

«"APK build is working"»

unless a successful workflow run has produced the APK artifact.

Repository safety

Before changing the repository:

- Inspect the existing branch.
- Inspect existing GitHub Actions workflows.
- Do not delete working workflows unnecessarily.
- Do not overwrite unrelated project configuration.
- Keep changes focused on VEYRA.
- Do not push to a different repository.
- Do not falsely claim a push occurred.

If the current repository has an existing CI/build system, integrate with it rather than creating conflicting workflows.

